WEBVTT

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.600
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:45.950
And takes me

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.900
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.050
To the home of wind

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.400
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.600
After the Christmas concert was over,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.650
the children of Avonlea school found it hard to
settle down to humdrum existence again.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:54.350
To Anne in particular things seemed
fearfully flat, stale, and unprofitable

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.660
after the goblet of excitement
she had been sipping for weeks.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:11.060
I'm certain that life can never be the same
again as it was in those olden days, Diana.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.850
...Said Anne mournfully, as if referring to
a period of at least fifty years back.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.000
Perhaps after a while I'll get used to it,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.980
but I'm afraid concerts spoil
people for everyday life.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.890
I suppose that is why
Marilla disapproves of them.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.700
Marilla is such a sensible woman.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.190
It must be a great deal better to be sensible.

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:40.180
But still, I don't believe I'd really
want to be a sensible person,

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.820
because they are so unromantic.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:47.470
Mrs. Lynde says there is no
danger of me ever being one.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.460
But... you can never tell.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:58.950
I feel just now that I may
grow up to be sensible yet.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.860
But perhaps that is only because I'm tired.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.980
I simply couldn't sleep
last night for ever so long.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:13.030
I just lay awake and imagined the
concert over and over again.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.800
That's one splendid thing about such affairs.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:22.690
It's so lovely to look back to them.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.190
Could Anne go back to the
former quiet pleasures

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.800
of those faraway days before the concert?

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:38.970
At first, as she told Diana,
she did not really think she could.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.480
Eventually, however, Avonlea school

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:53.050
slipped back into its old groove
and took up its old interests.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:57.700
To be sure, the concert left traces.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.070
Because of a quarrel over a point of
precedence in their platform seats,

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.120
Katie Andrews and Emma White
no longer sat at the same desk,

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.150
and a promising friendship of
three years was broken up.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.610
Josie Pye and Julia Bell did
not "speak" for three months,

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:21.230
because Josie Pye had told Bessie Wright

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:25.210
that Julia Bell's bow made her think
of a chicken jerking its head,

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.790
and Bessie told Julia.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:35.500
None of the Sloanes would have
any dealings with the Bells,

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.440
because the Bells had declared that the
Sloanes had too much to do in the program,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.470
and the Sloanes had retorted that the Bells

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.810
were not capable of doing the
little they had to do properly.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:52.970
Finally, Charlie Sloane fought
Moody Spurgeon MacPherson,

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:58.860
because Moody Spurgeon had said that
Anne Shirley put on airs about her recitations.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:02.870
Consequently Moody Spurgeon's
sister, Ella May,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.480
would not "speak" to Anne Shirley
all the rest of the winter.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.220
With the exception of these trifling frictions,

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:16.500
work in Miss Stacy's little kingdom went on
with regularity and smoothness.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.980
The winter weeks slipped by.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:26.480
It was an unusually mild winter,
with so little snow

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:33.230
that Anne and Diana could go to school
nearly every day by way of the Birch Path.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:39.350
On Anne's birthday they
were tripping lightly down it,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:44.550
keeping eyes and ears alert
amid all their chatter,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.940
for Miss Stacy had told them that
they must soon write a composition on

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:54.760
"A Winter's Walk in the Woods,"
and it behooved them to be observant.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:56.830
Footprints!

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.040
What could it be?

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.030
It must be a rabbit.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.030
Yes.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.450
They're still fresh.
It must be close.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.830
Just think, Diana,
I'm thirteen years old today.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:19.540
Oh, I'm sorry!
I completely forgot about it.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.370
Don't worry, it's all right.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.910
In two more years I'll be really grown up.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.030
It's a great comfort to think that

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.990
I'll be able to use big words then
without being laughed at.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:36.570
But I can scarcely realize that
I'm almost a grown up.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.950
When I woke this morning it seemed to me
that everything must be different

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.700
You've been thirteen for a month,

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:49.880
so I suppose it doesn't seem such
a novelty to you as it does to me.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.420
But It makes life seem so
much more interesting.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.670
Ruby Gillis says she means to have a
beau as soon as she's fifteen.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.030
Ruby Gillis thinks of nothing but beaus!

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:08.160
She's actually delighted when anyone
writes her name up in a take-notice

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.810
for all she pretends to be so mad.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.120
But I'm afraid that is an
uncharitable speech.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.630
Mrs. Allan says we should never
make uncharitable speeches,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:22.130
but they do slip out so often
before you think, don't they?

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:27.150
I simply can't talk about Josie Pye
without making an uncharitable speech,

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.580
so I never mention her at all.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:31.180
You may have noticed that.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:34.430
But you do mention her sometimes.

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:36.730
Oh!

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:41.400
I'm trying to be as much like
Mrs. Allan as I possibly can,

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.350
for I think she's perfect.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:46.870
Mr. Allan thinks so too.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:52.940
Mrs. Lynde says he just worships
the ground she treads on.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:01.230
And that it's not right for a minister to set
his affections so much on a mortal being.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:06.330
But then, Diana,

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:13.900
even ministers are human and have their
besetting sins just like everybody else.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:18.930
My besetting sin is imagining too much

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.490
and forgetting my duties.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:24.650
I'm striving very hard to overcome it

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.550
and now that I'm really thirteen
perhaps I'll get on better.

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:31.750
In four more years we'll
be able to put our hair up.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:36.100
Alice Bell is only sixteen and
she is wearing hers up.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.530
But I think that's ridiculous.
I shall wait until I'm seventeen.

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.970
If I had Alice Bell's crooked nose
I wouldn't...

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:52.570
I won't say what I was going to
because it was extremely uncharitable.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.490
Besides, I was comparing it with
my own nose and that's vanity.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:08:59.550
Oh, Anne!

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.520
I think too much about my nose ever since
I heard that compliment about it long ago.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:07.580
It really is a great comfort to me.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.750
But your nose is really beautiful.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:12.640
look, there's a rabbit!

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.180
I wonder if the footprints
from before belongs to it?

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:21.570
It can't be.

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:29.700
That's something to remember
for our woods composition.

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:30.700
Indeed.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:40.190
I really think the woods are just as
lovely in winter as in summer.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:45.980
They're so white and still, as if they were
asleep and dreaming pretty dreams.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:09.870
I won't mind writing that composition
when its time comes.

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:13.350
But the one we're to
hand in Monday is terrible.

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:19.240
The idea of Miss Stacy telling us to write
a story out of our own heads!

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.720
Why, it's as easy as wink.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.030
It's easy for you because
you have an imagination.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:30.730
But what would you do if you
had been born without one?

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.740
I suppose you have your composition all done?

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:37.880
I wrote it last Monday evening.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:43.750
It's called 'The Jealous Rival;
or In Death Not Divided.'

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:46.430
Oh, a romantic novel, isn't it?

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:51.950
But I read it to Marilla and she
said it was stuff and nonsense.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:57.570
Then I read it to Matthew
and he said it was fine.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:01.870
But it's a sad, sweet story.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.350
I...

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:08.100
...just cried like a child while I was writing it.

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:11.670
It's about...

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:19.390
It's about two beautiful maidens called
Cordelia Montmorency and Geraldine Seymour

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.980
who lived in the same village and were
devotedly attached to each other.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:31.520
Cordelia was a regal brunette
with a coronet of midnight hair

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.790
and duskly flashing eyes.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:38.850
Geraldine was a queenly blonde
with hair like spun gold

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:41.770
and velvety purple eyes.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:48.080
I never saw anybody with purple eyes.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.470
Neither did I.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.630
I just imagined them.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.690
I wanted something out of the common.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.980
Geraldine had an alabaster brow too.

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:05.770
Well, what became of Cordelia and Geraldine?

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:08.650
Anne! Anne!

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:15.570
How long do you intend to stand there?
You'll catch a cold!

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:16.920
Oh no!

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:19.890
I'll tell you next time.
See you tomorrow.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.110
Goodbye.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:23.090
Goodbye!

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:27.170
Cordelia and Geraldine...

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:33.640
Diana was beginning to feel
rather interested in their fate.

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:52.900
And then Geraldine and Cordelia grew in
beauty side by side until they were sixteen.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:57.830
Then Bertram DeVere
came to their native village

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.690
and fell in love with the fair Geraldine.

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.420
When her horse ran away with her...

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:28.370
...he saved her life.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:33.740
Geraldine fainted in his arms and he
carried her home three miles

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:37.360
because, the carriage was all smashed up.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:44.540
I found it rather hard to
imagine the proposal

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:47.450
because I had no experience to go by.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:52.340
I asked Ruby Gillis if she knew anything
about how men proposed.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:57.700
I thought she'd likely be an authority on the
subject, having so many sisters married.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:04.620
Ruby told me that when Malcolm Andres
proposed to her sister Susan

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:06.940
she was hidden in the hall pantry.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:13.980
She said Malcolm told Susan that his dad
had given him the farm in his own name

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:19.980
and then said, 'What do you say, darling pet,
if we get hitched this fall?'

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:21.510
And Susan said,

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:25.090
'Yes... no... I don't know... let me see'...

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:28.870
And there they were,
engaged as quick as that.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:36.570
But I didn't think that sort of a
proposal was a very romantic one,

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:41.140
so in the end I had to imagine it
out as well as I could.

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:47.200
I made it very flowery and poetical
and Bertram went on his knees.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:51.350
Although Ruby Gillis says
it isn't done nowadays.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:56.060
Geraldine accepted him in a speech a page long.

00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:59.300
I took a lot of trouble with that speech.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:02.980
I rewrote it five times and
I look upon it as my masterpiece.

00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:07.570
Bertram gave her a diamond ring
and a ruby necklace

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:10.800
and told her they would go to
Europe for a wedding tour,

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:13.280
for he was immensely wealthy.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:18.480
But then, alas, shadows began
to darken over their path.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:24.180
Cordelia was secretly in love
with Bertram herself.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:29.000
So when Geraldine told her about the
engagement she was simply furious,

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.570
especially when she saw the
necklace and the diamond ring.

00:15:35.580 --> 00:15:39.110
All her affection for Geraldine
turned to bitter hate

00:15:39.110 --> 00:15:42.950
and she vowed that she
should never marry Bertram.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:48.730
But she pretended to be
Geraldine's friend the same as ever.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:16:00.170
One evening they were standing on the
bridge over a rushing turbulent stream,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:04.020
and Cordelia, thinking they were alone...

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.000
...with a wild, mocking

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:17.170
pushed Geraldine over the brink.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.870
But Bertram saw it all and exclaimed:

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:29.230
'I will save thee, my peerless Geraldine.'

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:32.150
And then he plunged into the current.

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:38.530
But alas, he had forgotten he couldn't swim,

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:42.430
and they were both drowned,
clasped in each other's arms.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:50.850
Their bodies were washed ashore
soon afterwards.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:17:00.400
They were buried in the one grave and
their funeral was most imposing.

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:06.960
Diana, it's so much more romantic to end a
story up with a funeral than a wedding.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:19.590
Cordelia, overcome with remorse, leapt down
the same river they drowned in.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:25.580
I thought that was a poetical
retribution for her crime.

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:28.060
How perfectly lovely!

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:34.210
I don't see how you can make up such
thrilling things out of your own head.

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:39.900
Anne, I wish my imagination
was as good as yours.

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:42.780
It would be if you'd only cultivate it.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:43.780
I know!

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:45.850
I've just thought of a plan, Diana.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:50.400
Let you and me have a story club all our
own and write stories for practice.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:52.990
I'll help you along until you can
do them by yourself.

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:56.180
You ought to cultivate
your imagination, you know.

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:58.520
Miss Stacy says so.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:06.700
This was how the story club came into existence.

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:09.710
It was limited to Diana
and Anne at first,

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:13.170
but soon it was extended to
include Jane Andrews and Ruby Gillis

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:19.280
and one or two others who felt
that their imaginations needed cultivating.

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:22.360
No boys were allowed in it.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:27.010
Although Ruby Gillis opined that
their admission would make it more exciting.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:31.450
And each member
had to produce one story a week.

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:35.160
It's extremely interesting, Marilla.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:38.010
Each girl has to read her story out loud...

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:42.120
and then we talk it over.

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:47.260
We are going to keep them all sacredly and
have them to read to our descendants.

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.230
We each write under a nom-de-plume.

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:56.450
Mine is Rosamond Montmorency.

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:59.130
Aren't you Cordelia Fitzgerald?

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:02.510
No. I'm Rosamond Montmorency.

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:04.310
All the girls do pretty well.

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:06.700
Ruby Gillis is rather sentimental.

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.210
She puts too much lovemaking into her stories.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:15.830
Jane never puts any because she feels
silly when she has to read it out loud.

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:18.020
Jane's stories are extremely sensible.

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:23.720
Then Diana puts too many murders into hers.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.640
Most of the time she doesn't
know what to do with the people

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:29.080
so she kills them off to get rid of them.

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:37.910
I mostly always have to
tell them what to write about.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:46.000
I think this story-writing business
is the foolishest yet.

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:56.360
You'll get a pack of nonsense into your heads

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:59.870
and waste time that should
be put on your lessons.

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:09.130
But we're so careful to put
a moral into them all, Marilla.

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:18.220
All the good people are rewarded and
all the bad ones are suitably punished.

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:22.280
I'm sure that must have a wholesome effect.

00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:27.700
The moral is the great thing.
Mr. Allan says so.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:34.200
I read one of my stories to
him and Mrs. Allan.

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:37.430
They both agreed that
the moral was excellent.

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:38.650
Only...

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:43.810
...they laughed in the wrong places.

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:46.280
I like it better when people cry.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:53.020
Jane and Ruby almost always cry
when I come to the pathetic parts.

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:57.970
Diana wrote her Aunt Josephine
about our club

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:02.280
and she wrote back that we were
to send her some of our stories.

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:05.800
So we copied out four of our
very best and sent them.

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:12.360
Miss Josephine Barry wrote back that she had
never read anything so amusing in her life.

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:14.960
That kind of puzzled us

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:19.460
because the stories were all very
pathetic and almost everybody died.

00:21:19.460 --> 00:21:22.130
But I'm glad Miss Barry liked them.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:27.460
It shows our club
is doing some good in the world.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:33.120
Mrs. Allan says that ought to be our
object in everything.

00:21:33.430 --> 00:21:37.250
I hope I shall be a little like Mrs. Allan
when I grow up.

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:39.620
Do you think there
is any prospect of it, Marilla?

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.030
I shouldn't say there was a great deal.

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:53.060
I'm sure Mrs. Allan was never
such a silly, forgetful little girl as you are.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:54.410
That's true.

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:59.240
But she wasn't always
so good as she is now either.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:01.750
She said she was a dreadful mischief

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:04.020
when she was a girl and
was always getting into scrapes.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:07.140
I felt so encouraged when I heard that.

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:09.200
But Marilla,

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:14.570
is it very wicked of me to
feel encouraged when I hear

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:16.160
that other people have
been bad and mischievous?

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:18.580
Mrs. Lynde says it is.

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:24.520
She always feels shocked when she hears
of anyone ever having been naughty,

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:26.370
no matter how small they were.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:30.820
She once heard
a minister confess that when he was a boy

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:34.450
he stole a strawberry tart out of his aunt's pantry

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:37.880
and she never had any
respect for that minister again.

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:43.290
But I thought that it
was real noble of him to confess it.

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:47.990
Because it's encouraging
for small boys nowadays

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:52.890
who do naughty things and are sorry for them
to know that perhaps they may grow up

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:55.930
to be ministers in spite of it.

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:58.910
That's how I'd feel, Marilla.

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:02.470
The way I feel at present, Anne,

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:06.680
is that it's high time
you had those dishes washed.

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:10.840
You've taken half an hour longer than
you should with all your chattering.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:18.120
Learn to work first and talk afterwards.

00:24:17.890 --> 00:24:21.330
A strange salesperson sells
Anne hair dye that she uses

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:24.800
and despairs over her ugly hair!

00:24:24.940 --> 00:24:27.550
Next episode of Anne of Green Gables:

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:29.110
Vanity and Vexation of Spirit

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:30.170
Please watch!
